{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Premise VERIFIED before changing anything — it holds, on base b91f8f6830.\n\nStatic: `_private_key_env` (`src/rebar/opcert_service/config.py:79`) called\n`os.environ.get(\"REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY\")` and never removed it; `compose_signer` reads\n`cfg.private_key` through `_read_source_key` (`keyprov.py:79`) and never touches the\nenvironment, so nothing was unsetting it anywhere.\n\nEmpirical, with an obvious dummy value (never a real key): `from_env()` returned the value as\n`cfg.private_key` (True), `\"REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY\" in os.environ` afterwards was still\nTrue, and a child `subprocess.run` reported True as well. `repr(cfg)` did NOT leak it — the\nexisting `repr=False` on the field already covered that, so repr was never the hole.\n\nCallers of `OpcertServiceConfig.from_env` — the whole trace. EXACTLY ONE, in the entire tree:\n`src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py:171`, `app.state.config = OpcertServiceConfig.from_env()`, at\nMODULE scope. No test, script, or infra file calls it (`git grep \"OpcertServiceConfig.from_env\"`\nand `git grep \"\\.from_env()\"` over src/, tests/, scripts/, infra/ — every other hit is\n`LLMConfig` / `ReceiverConfig` / `LangfuseConfig` / `JanitorConfig`, different classes).\nExisting opcert tests construct `OpcertServiceConfig(...)` directly and are unaffected. The\ncontainer runs `uvicorn rebar.opcert_service.app:app` with neither `--workers` nor `--reload`\n(`infra/compose/Dockerfile.opcert:47`), so the module is imported once per process.\n\nSo there is NO config-reload path to break today. Rather than leave that as an assumption,\n`test_second_from_env_call_has_no_inline_key` pins consume-once as the contract: a second\n`from_env()` yields `private_key is None`. If someone later adds a reload path, that test is\nwhere it fails loudly, instead of the service silently composing without a key.\n\nWhat the hardening actually buys, precisely:\n- Removes the raw PEM from any in-process `os.environ` dump taken AFTER config load — a crash\n  handler, an error reporter, a debug endpoint, a traceback rendering the environment.\n- Removes it from the environment inherited by children spawned after config load:\n  `keyprov._validate_key_file`'s `ssh-keygen` (`keyprov.py:135`) and `workspace._git`'s `git`\n  (`workspace.py:73`), neither of which passes an explicit `env=`, so both inherit it today.\n\nWhat it does NOT buy (deliberately not overstated):\n- The key REMAINS in process memory — `cfg.private_key` is reachable for the process lifetime\n  through `app.state.config`, and `compose_signer` writes a 0600 copy to a process-owned dir.\n  A core dump or heap scrape is unaffected.\n- It retracts nothing that already read the variable, and nothing about the deployment\n  platform's own record of the injected value.\n- On Linux it does NOT scrub `/proc/<pid>/environ`, which reflects the exec-time environment\n  block that `unsetenv` does not rewrite.\nIt narrows the exposure window; it does not erase the secret. And the DEPLOYED path uses\n`REBAR_OPCERT_KEY_PATH` (`infra/compose/docker-compose.yml:285`; the inline variable appears\nnowhere under `infra/`), so this is hygiene on the secondary dev/compat source.\n\nRED then GREEN. RED on unmodified main, for the right reason: 4 failed, 2 passed, the lead\nfailure being `test_from_env_consumes_inline_key` -> \"REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY must be popped\nonce from_env has captured it\" (the variable still present), not an unrelated error. The 2\nthat passed are the unset-no-op and key_path-non-interference guards, which correctly hold on\nmain. GREEN after the change: 6 passed.\n\nTwo things worth recording that the naive fix got wrong:\n1. Replacing `os.environ.get(...)` with `os.environ.pop(...)` SILENTLY DROPPED\n   `REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY` from the generated `docs/env-vars.md` (161 -> 160 variables):\n   `scripts/gen_env_registry.py` recognizes only `os.environ[\"X\"]` / `os.environ.get(\"X\")` /\n   `os.getenv(\"X\")` / `KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS`. `make lint` does NOT catch this; the full suite\n   does, via `tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp_http_transport.py::test_generators_not_stale_and_include_new_keys`.\n   The read therefore stays an `os.environ.get` with a literal key and the pop is a separate\n   statement, with a comment saying why.\n2. The new docstring shifts a line number the registry records, so `docs/env-vars.md` is\n   regenerated here (`python scripts/gen_env_registry.py --check` exits 0).\n\nTest-hygiene detail: the first RED run's `assert _ENV not in os.environ` rendered the whole\n`os.environ` in pytest's failure output, dumping the throwaway key. The assertions now bind\nmembership to a bool first (`_env_present()`), so a failure can never dump the environment.\n\nGates, on the committed tree:\n- `make lint` -> pass (ruff check, ruff format --check 2171 files, config gates, zizmor, actionlint)\n- `make typecheck` -> Success: no issues found in 580 source files\n- `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` run DIRECTLY -> `active=49 new=0\n  increased=0 stale=0`\n- `python scripts/gen_env_registry.py --check` -> exit 0\n`.github/complexity-baseline.json` was not edited.\n\nScope held: config.py + the new test + the regenerated registry. No other credential-handling\nweakness was absorbed into this change.\n\nGerrit change 1944: https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/1944"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787198104786397001,"uuid":"b67fa1de-158e-4298-8f88-36a0e2bc28bb"}